theory rules_example
imports nat
description "Samples for the rules a `fun` expansion emits (case and elimination), in shapes the other theories do not carry."

-- The expansion emits the coverage theorem, the case rule, the
-- elimination rule and the induction rule for every definition.  The
-- library's own definitions exercise most of their shapes; the three
-- below are the ones nothing else reaches, and each of them is a place
-- the emitted proofs could go wrong on their own:
--
--   * two clauses with no pattern variable *before* one with a variable.
--     The elimination rule's branches then repeat their propositions
--     (nothing about them names the branch) and a step whose proposition
--     is already an item lays out no line at all -- so the literal IDs
--     after it are the ones to get wrong.
--   * two arguments and three clauses, one of which keeps the second
--     argument: the clauses do not all consume the same position, so the
--     relation is a two-column chain and the rules have to follow it.
--   * a boolean-valued definition, whose right hand sides are booleans:
--     the propositions print with `⟷` and are read back as equalities.

fun gz :: nat ⇒ nat
  | gz 0 = 0
  | gz (Suc 0) = 1
  | gz (Suc (Suc n)) = n

fun gr :: nat ⇒ nat ⇒ nat
  | gr 0 n = n
  | gr (Suc m) 0 = m
  | gr (Suc m) (Suc n) = gr m n

fun gb :: nat ⇒ bool
  | gb 0 ⟷ true
  | gb (Suc n) ⟷ false
